	abstract = {Product designs are optimized repeatedly with different versions and generations. However, when to initialize an upgraded design and which function or performance should be improved are not trivial problems. Based on affective design, this paper develops a dynamic Kansei engineering method, which uses time-series analysis method to investigate user sentiment's evolvement on different design features, where their sentiment is captured and quantified by a Bi-LSTM (Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory) natural language process model. An index called ADK (Augmented Dynamic Kansei) is proposed to represent the relative value of text sentiment of our brand and the benchmarking brand in a certain time interval, therefore trend stability and abrupt change points of ADK series can be monitored. A case study on vehicles' intelligent connected functions is presented, where 68,658 UGC (User-Generated Contents) from 65,689 users on 29 car models were collected from automobile ecosystem APPs and forums. Feedback from our cooperated automobile company indicates that the proposed methods are practical: user sentiment's trend and abrupt change points, which can be easily overestimated or overlooked by conventional methods, can help decision making on design management.  © 2022 IEEE.},
