	abstract = {Time delay estimation is an important task for process control, abnormality monitoring and fault diagnosis. For certain industrial systems, time delays may change from time to time, subject to variations of operating conditions. This paper proposes an approach to estimate variable time delays based on significant change points. The approach is composed of two main steps. First, significant change points are defined as the turning points that change from a steady-state segment to a change segment in one time series; the significant change points are found automatically by exploiting the piecewise linear representation (PLR) technique. Second, the context of significant change points is exploited as the query sequence Q; time delays are estimated based on the Pearson correlation coefficient between the query sequence and another time series. The simulation example of the Tennessee Eastman model illustrates the effectiveness of the proposed method and compares it with existing ones.  © 2021 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Pearson correlation coefficient; PLR; significant change point; Tennessee model; time delay},
