	abstract = {Anomaly detection in time series is an important task with several practical applications. The common approach of training one model in an offline manner using historical data is likely to fail under dynamically changing and non-stationary environments where the definition of normal behavior changes over time making the model irrelevant and ineffective. In this paper, we describe a temporal model based on Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) for time series anomaly detection to address challenges posed by sudden or regular changes in normal behavior. The model is trained incrementally as new data becomes available, and is capable of adapting to the changes in the data distribution. RNN is used to make multi-step predictions of the time series, and the prediction errors are used to update the RNN model as well as detect anomalies and change points. Large prediction error is used to indicate anomalous behavior or a change (drift) in normal behavior. Further, the prediction errors are also used to update the RNN model in such a way that short term anomalies or outliers do not lead to a drastic change in the model parameters whereas high prediction errors over a period of time lead to significant updates in the model parameters such that the model rapidly adapts to the new norm. We demonstrate the efficacy of the proposed approach on a diverse set of synthetic, publicly available and proprietary real-world datasets. © 2018 Association for Computing Machinery.},
