	abstract = {Modelling non-stationary time-series is a challenging but important task. One of the key issues we face is explaining such data with stationary parametric models without creating overly complex models. Identifying trends in time-varying data is a key step in simplifying such models and deconstructing signals into realizations of local models separated by change-points. We study the problem of simultaneous change-point detection and inference under a piece-wise linear model using switching Kalman filters. Under the piece-wise linear dynamics assumption we look at the problem of inference as a model selection task and try to find the most likely regime sequence in polynomial time. To motivate the utility of our model we illustrate our approach in a financial time-series analysis setting where we propose a method where exogenous data can be used in conjunction with time series data to identify change-points and test various hypotheses regarding the relation of exogenous data such as news articles mentioning a financial instrument to price fluctuations. © 2017 IEEE.},
