	abstract = {Toraja's local people cultivate the mountainous slope according to its topographic properties. However, during the past 3 decades, the livelihood system and the slope land-use of local people have been changed drastically, caused by the social impacts like road construction and commercialization. The aims of this paper are, firstly to find out the trends of vegetation change in each term, secondly to clarify the characteristic of vegetation change trends in each topographic and geographic condition and thirdly to consider the significant change with comparison of social impacts. Normalized Difference Vegetation Index was calculated by the Landsat images which were acquired in 1990, 2002, 2011 and 2013. Then the distance of each pixel were calculated in each three observation intervals. Then topographic variables were calculated by the ASTER GDEM image. By these topographic variables, topographic type was classified by the K-means classification. With the additional reclassified geographic factors as distance to road and elevation, topographic types were exported to the 20,000 random points. The trend of vegetation changes according to the topographic and geographic condition in three terms was examined by the Two-way repeated measures analysis of variance. The pairwise comparison clarified the existence of significant difference of vegetation change in every observed term. Tests of within-subjects effects showed the existence of interaction in each topographic and geographic factor at the significant level of 0.05. The test of the between-subjects groups as eight classed topography, four classed distance to road and elevation indicated the vegetation movement in topographic and geographic factors. Then we concluded relation of trends of vegetation change and the social impacts detected by the change point of social factors trends. However, for further understanding, it is necessary to verify the brought hypothesis by the further evidence by the field in