	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-84925363745&partnerID=40&md5=f6ea3996c4758e78fa961330d98d62ea},
	abstract = {Climate change and the consequential effects of more frequent extreme flood events may lead to the assumption of increasing discharge variability and flood risk. In this study, systematic investigations of discharges and the development of flood risk are carried out at gauge Worms (Rhine River) based on a data set going back to 1820. The key question to be answered is: do extreme events of the last years have an influence on the statistical properties of extreme values and the related flood risk? In a first step we analyse the discharge time series for periodic variations as well as trends in the monthly mean discharges. Significant negative trends are found in summer discharges (August and September) whereas the winter months (December - May) show positive trends. The variation of flood risk is computed for the 100-yr recurrence interval using a quasi non-stationary approach. After detrending the entire time series, the 100-yr recurrence interval is calculated from a time series from 1820 to 1940. The time series is then steadily enlarged by one year until the entire time series from 1820 to 2011 is analysed. Thus, a time series of the 100-yr return level from 1940 to 2011 is available and can be analysed e.g. in terms of change points or other non-stationary behavior. For calculating the 100-yr recurrence intervals, the Generalized Pareto distribution (GPD) is applied to peak-over-threshold- series (POT), considering thresholds μ+1.4σ, μ +1.8σ, and μ +2.5σ. For comparison, the Generalized Extreme Value distribution (GEV) is also applied, using the r-largest approach with r = 1 (i.e. annual maxima), r = 2 (i.e. two largest values per year), and r = 3 (i.e. three largest values per year). The results show that single extreme events cause sudden rises in the flood risk series no matter which distribution and method is considered. © 2012 Engineers Australia.},
