	abstract = {Three hundred and ninety eight long-term time series of maximal annual flow discharges from USA, Canada, Poland, and Hawaii are analyzed by means of non-parametric statistical tests to find whether they can be considered homogeneous. The following tests are applied: runs test of independence of elements in the series, Mann's test of trend in the mean and in the variance, and Lombard's and Pettitt's tests of the number and locations of the change points in the mean value. The verification of the above hypotheses has been carried out for 5% significance level. The hypotheses of independent elements has been rejected in 13.1% of series, the homogeneity of the mean value in 19.6% of cases mostly showing an increasing trend, and the homogeneity of the variance in 6%. Sneyers' proposal of the sequential use of the Mann test has been compared with the original Mann test both on real and synthetic data with the final conclusion that it can be used for testing homogeneity of series as long as the distribution of the respective statistic is derived.},
